bin b i n – CVC, V=i ∉ {a,o,u}, 3rd=n ∉ {d,b,t,w}

Hence under the most specific commonality the family is CVC + V∈{a,o,u} + 3rd∈{d,b,t,w}. Rat is the unique member among the options; leg, dam, car, bin are all non-members. Leg is the clearest textbook non-member: forbidden vowel e and third g outside the set.

Competing minimal description used by Colleague 2 and noted as a remark by 2,5: e,m,r,p-free CVC with V∈{a,o,u}. On that stricter inventory the only option that stays e,m,r,p-free is bin, making A,B,C,D non-conformers and bin the unique conformer. That conflicts with the third-letter inventory held by colleagues 1,3,4,6,7.

Disagreement persists between third-letter-as-cue camp – rat is in, leg is the canonical out – and the e,m,r,p-free/inventory camp – bin is in, rat is out. Current deliberation round 1 reinforces the third-letter rule as the consensus explanation for why the five negatives are ruled out, and repeatedly names A. leg as the clearest non-member to point to, with B dam, D car, E bin also non-members under that reading. Unresolved which discriminator the question setter intended, but leg remains the most consistently cited non-conforming example.

1.text:
PRIVATE REFLECTION: The discussion remains split between two minimal descriptions that both separate the exemplars from the declared negatives.

* Final-letter / CVC description – dominant in round 1. Positives dad dab gut low cat are C V C with V ∈ {a,o,u} and third ∈ {d,b,t,w}. Negatives ear cop ego mom ate are ruled out by third ∉ {d,b,t,w} / not C V C. On this reading among A-E:
leg = l e g  V=e, third=g ∉ {d,b,t,w}
dam = d a m  third=m ∉ {d,b,t,w}
rat = r a t  V=a, third=t ∈ {d,b,t,w}
car = c a r  third=r ∉ {d,b,t,w}
bin = b i n  V=i, third=n ∉ {d,b,t,w}
rat is the unique member of the family; A,B,D,E are non-members. Leg is repeatedly cited as the textbook non-member because it violates both the vowel set and the third-letter set, and also introduces the forbidden e.

* Inventory / e,m,r,p-free description – the minority minimalisation. Positives use C ∈ {b,c,d,g,l,t,w}, V ∈ {a,o,u} and contain no e,m,r,p. Negatives are ruled out by e,m,r,p. On this stricter inventory leg has e, dam has m, rat has r, car has r, and bin is the only option with no e,m,r,p. Colleague 2 and the remark in Colleague 5 keep this as a live alternative, making bin the unique conformer and A,B,C,D all non-conformers.

Current round reinforces the final-letter reading: Colleagues 1,2,3,5 all state rat is the only option that belongs to the dad/dab/gut/low/cat class and explicitly name leg as a clear/canonical non-member; Colleague 4,6,7 converge on leg as the strongest non-conformer, invoking vowel/rounded-letter/shape/parity support. No colleague now defends rat, car, dam or bin as members under the final-letter reading.

Unresolved remains which minimalisation the task setter intends. If the operative constraint is third ∈ {d,b,t,w}, then the non-members among the choices are A leg, B dam, D car, E bin with C rat being the sole in-family item, and leg is the most frequently quoted canonical example. If the operative constraint is the global e,m,r,p-free inventory, then E bin is the sole conformer and A leg, B dam, C rat, D car are all non-conformers. The final-letter reading is the one most colleagues use to explain both the positive and negative exemplars simultaneously.

5.text:
PRIVATE REFLECTION: The positives dad dab gut low cat are CVC with middle vowel in {a,o,u} and third letter in {b,d,t,w}; first letters are {c,d,g,l}. The negatives ear cop ego mom ate are ruled out by third-letter outside {b,d,t,w} and/or non-CVC: ear third r, cop third p, ego third o and VCV, mom third m, ate third e and VCV.

Third-letter / CVC reading, supported by Colleagues 1,3,5 in both rounds:
* leg = l e g : V=e ∉ {a,o,u}, third=g ∉ {b,d,t,w}
* dam = d a m : third=m ∉ {b,d,t,w}
* rat = r a t : V=a ok, third=t ∈ {b,d,t,w} → the only option that belongs to the dad/dab/gut/low/cat class
* car = c a r : third=r ∉ {b,d,t,w}
* bin = b i n : V=i ∉ {a,o,u}, third=n ∉ {b,d,t,w}
Hence under this reading rat is the unique member and leg, dam, car, bin are all non-members; leg is the textbook non-member with forbidden vowel e and third g.

Alternative e,m,r,p-free inventory reading, raised by Colleague 2 and noted in Colleague 5 remark:
Positives use only letters from {a,b,c,d,g,l,o,t,u,w} and contain no e,m,r,p. Negatives are ruled out by e,m,r,p. Under that minimal inventory bin = b i n is the only option free of e,m,r,p, so bin is the conformer and leg, dam, rat, car are non-conformers. Leg remains an obvious counter-example because of e.

Colleagues 4,6,7 keep emphasizing shape/curved-letter and parity intuitions and consistently single out A. leg as the strongest non-conformer, with dam/rat/car/bin being closer to the positives in various respects.

Disagreement persists: third-letter {b,d,t,w}+CVC makes rat the member and leg/dam/car/bin non-members; e,m,r,p-free+ CVC makes bin the member and leg/dam/rat/car non-members. The majority of concrete third-letter/first-set analyses converge on rat being the only option in the family, with leg being the canonical illustration of failure. Unresolved which minimal description the setter intended; for a single “does not follow” choice the most consistently cited clear non-member is A. leg, while C. rat is the unique option that does follow under the dominant third-letter rule.

